activities.content:
Up until now, you might have noticed we used slightly artificial parameter names like `sName` or `pPrice` to avoid collisions with fields `name` and `price`:

```java
// The awkward beginner approach:
Student(String sName, int sAge) {
    name = sName;
    age = sAge;
}
```

In professional Java, developers prefer naming parameters with the real, descriptive property names: `name` and `age`.

### The Shadowing Trap:
Watch what happens if you write this without `this`:
```java
class Student {
    String name;

    Student(String name) {
        name = name; // ⚠️ DANGER: Assigns the parameter to itself!
    }
}

Student s = new Student("Rahul");
System.out.println(s.name); // Prints: null!
```
Why did it print `null`? Because inside the constructor, the local parameter `name` has higher priority than the field `name`. `name = name` simply reads the local parameter and writes it back to the local parameter, doing nothing to the object's field!

### The Professional Solution:
```java
class Student {
    String name;
    int age;

    Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name; // this.name = instance field, name = parameter
        this.age = age;   // this.age = instance field, age = parameter
    }
}
```

### Visual Diagram of `this`:
```text
Heap Object (0x7F2B)
┌────────────────────────────┐
│ name: "Rahul"  ◄───────────┼──── this.name = name ("Rahul")
│ age:  20       ◄───────────┼──── this.age = age (20)
└────────────────────────────┘
      ▲
      │
    'this' points right here to the current instance!
```

activities.codeSnippet:
class Account {
    String holder;
    double balance;

    // Using 'this' cleanly disambiguates fields from parameters
    Account(String holder, double balance) {
        this.holder = holder;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    void printSummary() {
        // 'this' is optional when there is no name collision,
        // but this.holder and holder mean the same thing here!
        System.out.println("Account: " + this.holder + " | Balance: $" + this.balance);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Account acc = new Account("Maya Patel", 1500.0);
        acc.printSummary();
    }
}

